            "text": "{\n  \"analysis\": \"The data has `dt=0.01` (100 Hz sampling rate). The channels are DPE, DPN, DPZ (East, North, Vertical). The waveform length is 12000 samples (120 seconds). \\n\\nFor phase picking:\\n- P-wave is typically most prominent on the vertical channel (DPZ, index 2).\\n- S-wave is typically most prominent on the horizontal channels (DPE, DPN, indices 0 and 1).\\n\\nSince I need a good F1 score (\u003e=0.7 for P, \u003e=0.6 for S), I should use a reliable algorithm. STA/LTA is a good start. I can also use Kurtosis for better precision. Given the constraints and the nature of the task, I will implement a basic STA/LTA picker and refine it.\",\n  \"plan\": \"1. Create a Python script `picker.py` that:\\n    a. Loads each file.\\n    b. Implements an STA/LTA algorithm.\\n    c. Applies STA/LTA to the Vertical channel for P-waves and a combined horizontal energy for S-waves.\\n    d. Selects the best picks based on thresholds.\\n    e. Writes the results to `/root/results.csv`.\\n2. Run the script.\\n3. Verify the output format.\",\n  \"commands\": [\n    {\n      \"keystrokes\": \"cat \u003c\u003c 'EOF' \u003e /root/picker.py\\nimport numpy as np\\nimport os\\nimport pandas as pd\\n\\ndef recursive_sta_lta(sig, nsta, nlta):\\n    sta = np.zeros(len(sig))\\n    lta = np.zeros(len(sig))\\n    # Simple STA/LTA implementation\\n    sq_sig = sig**2\\n    for i in range(len(sig)):\\n        if i \u003c nlta:\\n            sta[i] = np.mean(sq_sig[max(0, i-nsta):i+1])\\n            lta[i] = np.mean(sq_sig[0:i+1])\\n        else:\\n            sta[i] = np.mean(sq_sig[i-nsta:i+1])\\n            lta[i] = np.mean(sq_sig[i-nlta:i+1])\\n    return sta / (lta + 1e-10)\\n\\ndef fast_sta_lta(sig, nsta, nlta):\\n    sq_sig = sig**2\\n    sta = np.convolve(sq_sig, np.ones(nsta)/nsta, mode='same')\\n    lta = np.convolve(sq_sig, np.ones(nlta)/nlta, mode='same')\\n    return sta / (lta + 1e-10)\\n\\ndef pick_phases():\\n    data_dir = '/root/data/'\\n    files = sorted([f for f in os.listdir(data_dir) if f.endswith('.npz')])\\n    results = []\\n    \\n    for f in files:\\n        path = os.path.join(data_dir, f)\\n        d = np.load(path)\\n        data = d['data'] # (12000, 3)\\n        dt = d['dt']\\n        \\n        # Channels: DPE, DPN, DPZ (usually)\\n        # P-wave: use Vertical channel (index 2)\\n        # S-wave: use Horizontal channels (index 0, 1)\\n        \\n        # Preprocessing: detrend and bandpass (simple highpass here)\\n        # But let's try STA/LTA on raw first, or just mean-removed\\n        data = data - np.mean(data, axis=0)\\n        \\n        # P-picking on DPZ\\n        p_sig = data[:, 2]\\n        p_char = fast_sta_lta(p_sig, int(0.1/dt), int(2.0/dt))\\n        p_picks = np.where(p_char \u003e 3.0)[0]\\n        if len(p_picks) \u003e 0:\\n            # Take the first pick that satisfies the threshold\\n            # Or better, the first local maximum above threshold\\n            results.append({'file_name': f, 'phase': 'P', 'pick_idx': p_picks[0]})\\n            \\n        # S-picking on Horizontals\\n        s_sig = np.sqrt(data[:, 0]**2 + data[:, 1]**2)\\n        s_char = fast_sta_lta(s_sig, int(0.2/dt), int(4.0/dt))\\n        # Avoid picking S before P or too close to P\\n        p_idx = p_picks[0] if len(p_picks) \u003e 0 else 0\\n        s_char[:p_idx + int(0.2/dt)] = 0\\n        s_picks = np.where(s_char \u003e 2.5)[0]\\n        if len(s_picks) \u003e 0:\\n            results.append({'file_name': f, 'phase': 'S', 'pick_idx': s_picks[0]})\\n            \\n    df = pd.DataFrame(results)\\n    df.to_csv('/root/results.csv', index=False)\\n\\nif __name__ == '__main__':\\n    pick_phases()\\nEOF\\n\",\n      \"duration\": 1.0\n    },\n    {\n      \"keystrokes\": \"python3 /root/picker.py\\n\",\n      \"duration\": 10.0\n    },\n    {\n      \"keystrokes\": \"head -n 10 /root/results.csv\\n\",\n      \"duration\": 0.1\n    }\n  ]\n}"
